40,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"The public beds are a flat 1,100 yen, which is predictable. However, whole-building lets are risky because non-refundable deposits mean any last-minute cancellations will increase the cost for everyone else. We must be careful.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"The workload is a significant factor. Last year, the cooking duty took two hours daily from the organizers. Choosing a place with professional kitchen staff would prevent such an unfair burden on a few individuals.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,1,talk,Minako,,"My research shows that mixing year-groups happens primarily during shared evenings in the same accommodation. Also, a third of our members struggle with early arrivals, meaning overnight stays are a necessity for many.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"I found that inland centers often have very crowded rooms, with up to eight people per room and strict lights-out policies. This could be quite disruptive to those who need a quiet environment to truly rest.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"The transport costs vary wildly depending on the distance, which suggests that a flat fee might be unfair if we choose a far destination. Also, our own hall is too crowded to be viable.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Toshio, if we want to avoid the cooking burden and the financial risk of whole-building lets, public beds seem the most rational choice. They offer a predictable cost and likely professional management.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Ryuji, public beds are a start, but we must confirm they include meal services. If we still have to cook, the burden remains. We need a destination that minimizes the organizers' labor.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,2,talk,Minako,,"@Toshio, if we prioritize professional meal services and shared sleeping quarters, we can solve both the labor issue and the social fragmentation. We need a place where everyone stays together to ensure the year-groups actually interact.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Minako, while shared quarters encourage interaction, we must be careful. If the rooms are too crowded, as I suspect they are at some centers, the lack of quality sleep could negatively impact our training performance.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Ryuji @Toshio, if we opt for public beds, we must clarify how the flat fee covers transport. Since costs fluctuate so much by distance, a fixed price could unfairly penalize some members.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Asuka, I agree. To avoid another deficit, we should calculate transport costs separately or use a tiered system. We cannot afford to guess the budget and hope for the best this time.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Ryuji, @Asuka, a tiered system is acceptable. However, my priority remains the labor. If we choose a location that requires the organizers to handle meals, we are simply repeating last year's mistake. We must prioritize facilities with staff.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,3,talk,Minako,,"@Toshio, I agree that staff-managed meals are essential. However, we must also ensure the layout doesn't isolate people. A facility with a common area and shared rooms is the only way to break the year-group barriers.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Minako, I understand the desire for interaction, but we must balance it with the need for recovery. If we are forced into overcrowded rooms, the resulting exhaustion may hinder the very social bonds you wish to build.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Jonathan, your point about recovery is valid. However, we must also address the financial risk. If we choose a distant facility to avoid crowding, the transport costs will spike, further complicating the fairness of the flat fee.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Ryuji,,"We must prioritize the public beds for their cost predictability. To address @Asuka's concern, we will calculate transport separately. This removes the financial risk and ensures we don't end up with another deficit in the club account.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"@Ryuji, that solves the budget, but we must ensure the public beds we select provide full meal services. I will not support any option that forces the organizers back into the kitchen for two hours a day.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,2,4,talk,Minako,,"I agree with @Toshio on the meals. However, we must prioritize a facility where everyone stays together. If we split the group across different buildings or hotels, we will fail to integrate the year-groups again.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree that we need a unified space, but we must ensure it is not overcrowded. If we prioritize a facility with professional staff and reasonable room capacities, we can achieve both social harmony and proper rest.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,2,4,talk,Asuka,,"I agree with the need for professional staff and reasonable capacity. However, we must formally decide how to handle last-minute cancellations. If we commit to a booking, we need a clear rule on who covers the shortfall.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
